# 
# ## 5. Repository Structure and Team Collaboration
# 
# ### 5.1 Repository Purpose and Planned Organization
# 
# We will use our [Champions Athletic Map repository](https://github.com/SamTavasoli/champion-athletic-map) to maintain the code, documentation, and records needed to understand and reproduce our work. The master notebook will explain each development step and its findings. Reusable Python modules will provide the implementations shared by the notebook, application, and experiment runner.
# 
# We will introduce the following organization as we implement each component. This table describes our intended structure; some directories and configuration files will be added later.
# 
# | Location | Purpose |
# |---|---|
# | `notebooks/` | Master notebook and supporting experiments |
# | `src/champion_athletic_map/` | Shared data preparation, classification, retrieval, planning, and evaluation functions |
# | `app/` | Application interface using the Version 4 workflow |
# | `data/raw/` | Original source files retained unchanged when available |
# | `data/processed/` | Prepared task-specific datasets and versioned catalogs |
# | `data/references/` | Source snapshots, coaching material, planning guidance, and review records |
# | `data/synthetic/` | Existing fictional profiles and development examples |
# | `data/evaluation/` | Defined development and final-test partitions, expected behaviors, and scoring definitions |
# | `data/source_manifest.csv` | Source locations, versions, acquisition details, usage terms, and preparation notes |
# | `artifacts/` | Saved classifiers, preprocessing objects, label mappings, and retrieval indexes |
# | `results/` | Run records, audits, metrics, comparison tables, and figures |
# | `tests/` | Focused checks for shared functions, planning rules, and application behavior |
# | `docs/` | Technical notes, review packages, and report and presentation materials |
# | `README.md` | Project purpose, setup, required inputs, and execution instructions |
# | `pyproject.toml` and `requirements.txt` | Package configuration and tested dependency versions |
# | `.gitignore` and `.env.example` | File exclusions and configuration placeholders |
# 
# Paths will be resolved relative to a detected project root. We will document how to launch notebooks and scripts without relying on a particular computer or working directory.
# 
# ### 5.2 Integrating Existing Data and Code
# 
# We will review the existing dataset package, cleaning scripts, preparation notebooks, and locally saved outputs before incorporating them into the shared workflow. These materials cover exercise and food tables, contextual basketball records, drill catalogs and source evidence, recovery guidance, and fictional athlete profiles.
# 
# The imported package uses the existing path `Champions_Athletic_Agent_Ready_Dataset _Package/`. We will preserve that path while checking dependencies and documenting its contents. The master agent-ready file will serve as a heterogeneous record index; model training and reference preparation will use the relevant task-specific tables.
# 
# For each reused input, we will record its source, purpose, schema, transformations, and review status. Where original raw files are unavailable, we will identify the available export as the starting input and document the resulting reproduction limits.
# 
# We will distinguish processed data, reviewed metadata, catalog approval, and planner eligibility through explicit records. Reference indexing will use the approved subset defined for the experiment. Evaluation profiles, expected answers, and independent scoring records will remain outside retrieval.
# 
# As we consolidate notebook code, we will preserve source files, stable record identifiers, and historical review decisions. We will compare replacement outputs with the saved evidence before retiring older implementations or generated files. Any movement of profile files will preserve their identifiers and development or final-test assignments.
# 
# ### 5.3 Collaboration and Review
# 
# We will agree on the expected result of a task, protect uncommitted local work, and review incoming changes before integrating them. Focused branches and pull requests will describe what changed, why it was needed, and how we checked it. Reviewed changes will be merged into `main`.
# 
# We will coordinate edits to the master notebook and keep reusable logic in shared modules. Each notebook step will introduce its purpose and inputs, call the relevant implementation, and report the results needed to assess that step.
# 
# Reviews will consider correctness, readability, source traceability, evaluation boundaries, and compatibility with existing outputs. Comments will explain the work in plain language. Printed output will focus on findings, validation results, and issues that need attention. Observations will summarize completed work and its limitations after we inspect the results.
# 
# ### 5.4 Versioning and Reproduction
# 
# We will track source code, notebooks, configuration, tests, documentation, and suitable small data and reference files. Large datasets, models, and indexes will use a documented storage location with version identifiers or checksums and clear retrieval instructions. Required artifacts must be available to reproduce a reported result, subject to their source usage terms.
# 
# Before relocating an existing file, we will verify its dependencies and update the documented paths. Local environments, temporary files, caches, and real credentials will remain outside version control. Step 6 will define environment and credential configuration.
# 
# Each reported experiment will link the code revision, input and reference versions, split assignments, model and prompt settings, environment record, and saved outputs. Our README will explain the execution order and distinguish reproducing saved results from refreshing sources, preparing data, training models, or running new experiments.
# 
# We will update these records as the implementation develops so that another team member can reproduce the work and understand which decisions remain open.
# 
