# 
# 
# ## 6. Python Environment, Dependencies, and Configuration
# 
# ### 6.1 Development Environment
# 
# We will use VS Code with Python and Jupyter support for local development. Our starting environment will use Python 3.12 and a `.venv` directory inside the project. We will record the exact Python release and select that environment as the notebook kernel.
# 
# The environment check will identify our project from its notebook and data directories, confirm the active interpreter, and retain `project_root` as a shared `Path` for later steps. An unrecognized directory or a different kernel will require attention before we continue.
# 
# ### 6.2 Dependencies and Installation Records
# 
# We will check the packages needed for the next development stages:
# 
# | Purpose | Packages |
# |---|---|
# | Notebook execution | `ipykernel` |
# | Data preparation | `pandas`, `numpy` |
# | Plots and exploratory analysis | `matplotlib`, `seaborn` |
# | Classification baselines | `scikit-learn` |
# | Configuration | `PyYAML`, `python-dotenv` |
# | Automated checks | `pytest` |
# | Neural classifier | `torch`, with device and execution checks in Step 7 |
# 
# For the foundational packages, we will record installed versions and test imports. We will inspect PyTorch's installation metadata here and assess its operation in Step 7. Retrieval, language-model, and application packages will be added when we select their implementations.
# 
# We will maintain `pyproject.toml` for package configuration and `requirements.txt` for tested dependency versions. Installation instructions will identify any platform-specific or accelerator-specific requirements. Missing files that are still planned will be reported separately from environment failures.
# 
# ### 6.3 Shared Configuration and Reproducibility
# 
# We will keep paths, data partitions, model identifiers, training settings, retrieval options, generation parameters, and execution limits separate from program logic. Each experiment will preserve the settings and artifact versions used for that run.
# 
# Random seeds will be set in the steps that split data, create fictional profiles, or train models. We will save split assignments as well as seeds. Hardware, package versions, and nondeterministic operations can affect reproduction, so a shared seed alone will not establish identical results.
# 
# Credentials will come from environment variables or an excluded local `.env` file. We will provide placeholders in `.env.example`. Environment inspection will check file presence without loading credentials or displaying their values.
# 
# ### 6.4 Verification and Next Checkpoint
# 
