# 
# 
# ## 9. Dataset Loading and Initial Quality Review
# 
# ### 9.1 Purpose and Inputs
# 
# We will inspect the available datasets before further cleaning, exploratory analysis, and model development. We will examine file identity, table structure, missing information, duplicate records, identifiers, numerical measurements, and documented relationships.
# 
# Our starting inputs will be the source register and completed Step 8 audit. The number of registered sources can grow as we document additional material. Historical inputs, external references, prepared tables, and later catalog outputs will retain their separate roles.
# 
# Most of the files available at this checkpoint are prepared datasets. Missing historical publisher files and earlier fictional-profile inputs will remain documented provenance gaps. We will inspect the available material without treating it as a reconstruction of every original source.
# 
# ### 9.2 File Identity and Loading
# 
# We will reuse the project path and shared inspection functions established earlier. The current source register, Step 8 file inventory, and audit summary must agree before loading begins.
# 
# For each available file, we will compare its checksum with the latest Step 8 observation. We will also retain the comparison with its historical registration hash. This distinction will let us examine a saved later output while preserving its earlier version record.
# 
# We will verify table dimensions and compare row counts with the Step 8 inventory. CSV identifiers and source values will initially remain text. JSON records will retain their original value types, with checks for repeated object keys and nonfinite numeric values.
# 
# Large CSV files will be read row by row. Duplicate fingerprints and identifier sets will grow with the number of records; smaller task-specific tables will be retained for domain review. The complete master table will be inspected through streaming.
# 
# Catalog outputs from Steps 10–12 will be optional when they have not yet been produced. Available copies will receive the relevant integrity and structural checks. Missing historical inputs and external references will remain visible in the inventory.
# 
# ### 9.3 General Quality Measures
# 
# | Check area | What we will record |
# |---|---|
# | Structure | Readability, headers, row widths, required columns, and JSON record shapes |
# | Dimensions | Independently observed rows and columns, with count comparisons |
# | Identifiers | Missing or repeated identifiers where the schema requires uniqueness |
# | Duplicate screening | Repeated full-row fingerprints and relevant normalized-name matches |
# | Missing information | Missing cells by column, explicit unknown values, and literal absence labels |
# | Categories | Exercise labels, profile scenarios, catalog scope, and other relevant distributions |
# | Numerical values | Parseability, finiteness, documented ranges, and unusual measurements |
# | Relationships | Drill-to-document correspondence and prepared-file links in the master |
# 
# We will count cells, records, files, columns, and links separately. A missing-cell count will not be reported as the number of affected records.
# 
# Blank values and common null-like markers will be screened consistently. Explicit `unknown` values will remain separate. Literal `none` in injury-status or equipment fields can describe reported absence, so we will preserve that distinction. Numerical zero will remain a measured value rather than a missing-value substitute.
# 
# Missing optional information will require interpretation according to its purpose. For example, absent exercise ratings, blank profile scenario notes, and conditionally unused recovery bounds have different implications.
# 
# ### 9.4 Review by Data Family
# 
# | Data family | Initial checks | Work that follows |
# |---|---|---|
# | Exercises | Names, descriptions, body-part labels, category balance, and normalized-name screening | Text preparation and label review in Step 10 |
# | Fictional profiles | Identifiers, synthetic markers, integer ages and availability, categories, scenarios, and planning-field coverage | Profile expansion and independent final-test preparation |
# | Food and nutrition | Food identifiers, missing measurements, finite numbers, negative values, energy-method consistency, and preparation-report counts | Source-unit verification and documented reference preparation |
# | Basketball records | Metadata, identifiers, source-occurrence relationships, name overlaps, and eligibility consistency | Source and candidate reconciliation in Step 12 |
# | Recovery references | Source fields and conditional rule bounds, segments, and recorded status | Interpretation and applicability review in Step 13 |
# | Historical basketball tables | Structure, missing information, and full-row duplicate screening | Optional analysis with a documented project purpose |
# 
# For exercise classification, body-part labels will remain separate from derived goal tags. Missing descriptions and imbalanced categories will inform later modeling decisions rather than trigger automatic deletion or relabeling.
# 
# The fictional profiles will remain development examples. Age and availability checks will describe their recorded generator conventions. We will also record whether their schemas contain grade, session duration, existing commitments, sleep information, and participation history. Successful loading will not establish that the final benchmark has been prepared.
# 
# For nutrition, we will distinguish three measures: core fields containing values, core fields containing finite numbers, and core fields containing finite nonnegative numbers. Negative measurements and missing energy values will remain available for investigation. Column names alone will not establish verified units or reference quantities.
# 
# Basketball name matches will identify review candidates without merging activities. Matching RAG identifiers will establish document correspondence without approving the coaching content. Eligibility encodings will be interpreted explicitly, and an eligible record must carry an explicit approved catalog status. This consistency check will not replace the complete release review.
# 
# Recovery fields will be checked according to their declared rule type. Maximum, minimum, range, and exact-value records require different bounds. Age or grade pairs will be checked when supplied, while general rules may omit both. These checks will not activate rules or determine individual suitability.
# 
# ### 9.5 Master Dataset and Prepared-Row Links
# 
# We will inspect the master as a heterogeneous collection of prepared records. Its declared parents will define the domain tables used for comparison.
# 
# We will check unique master identifiers, known entity types, source filenames, valid JSON objects, and per-domain counts. The `source_row` field will be checked as a one-based position in the cleaned table, including position coverage and repeated links.
# 
# This positional check will not establish an original publisher row number. We will also leave full payload-value equality outside this inspection until we define how numeric formatting and omitted missing values should be compared.
# 
# We will preserve the master and its component tables. Additional datasets will remain separate until their integration has a documented purpose and transformation.
# 
# ### 9.6 Findings, Failure Handling, and Outputs
# 
# | Status | Meaning |
# |---|---|
# | `pass` | The implemented check found no violation of its defined condition |
# | `needs_review` | A quality condition or uncertainty requires investigation |
# | `failed` | A defined structural or integrity condition was violated |
# | `not_checked` | The stated review remains outside the implemented checks |
# | `not_applicable` | The check does not apply at this checkpoint |
# 
# Required failures will include unusable inputs, broken schemas or identifier contracts, invalid required JSON structures, broken master or RAG correspondence, incorrect synthetic labeling, and unapproved records marked eligible. We will save diagnostic reports and raise an error before continuing.
# 
# Incomplete descriptions, unusual nutrient values, profile coverage gaps, conditional rule questions, and name overlaps will remain review findings. We will preserve source values and record the action needed for each finding.
# 
# Our outputs will be:
# 
# | Output | Purpose |
# |---|---|
# | `results/data_inventory.csv` | File identity, dimensions, loading status, and checksum comparisons |
# | `results/initial_quality_checks.csv` | Every implemented check, its outcome, requirement level, and affected unit |
# | `results/column_quality_profile.csv` | Missing, unknown, and literal absence counts by column |
# | `results/data_category_counts.csv` | Relevant category distributions |
# | `results/data_quality_examples.csv` | Up to five record or group examples per finding, using prepared-record positions where applicable |
# | `results/step09_quality_summary.json` | Run scope, input-document hashes, domain metrics, and final outcome |
# 
# Generated reports will be updated with backups and restoration handling. The source register, review decisions, and dataset files will remain unchanged. Rerunning the cells will replace the generated reports without accumulating duplicate check rows.
# 
# We will print every flagged check in a compact form and finalize the observations after reviewing the local output. Step 10 will then address exercise preparation, with the other findings carried to their relevant review steps.
# 
# ### 9.7 Implementation References
# 
# - [Python CSV reading and writing](https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/csv.html)
# - [Python JSON decoding](https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/json.html)
# - [Finite-number checks](https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/math.html#math.isfinite)
