# ### Observations — Step 11: Exploratory Data Analysis and Data Summary
# 
# We examined the prepared exercise catalog and supporting reference data to understand their coverage, quality, relationships, and limitations. Our analysis identified several considerations for later classification, retrieval, and planning work while keeping source records, review decisions, and dataset roles separate.
# 
# #### 1. Input Verification and Analysis Scope
# 
# We verified 2,909 prepared exercise records and loaded ten supporting tables. The exercise artifacts matched the current Step 10 producer record.
# 
# Our source context included 23 local files inspected in Step 9. Nine historical input files remained unavailable in the checked locations.
# 
# These checks confirmed consistency with the recorded local inputs. They did not resolve the missing historical sources, independently verify every source statement, or establish permission and application suitability.
# 
# We treated earlier basketball catalogs as historical snapshots and reported them separately from the imported activities and current exercise catalog.
# 
# #### 2. Exercise Class Coverage and Imbalance
# 
# The exercise catalog retained 17 source body-part labels, with substantial differences in class size.
# 
# | Measure | Result |
# |---|---:|
# | Exercise records | 2,909 |
# | Source body-part labels | 17 |
# | Largest class: abdominals | 660 |
# | Second-largest class: quadriceps | 645 |
# | Smallest class: neck | 8 |
# | Largest-to-smallest class ratio | 82.50 |
# | Share represented by the two largest classes | 44.86% |
# 
# The full-catalog majority reference was 660 of 2,909 records, or 22.69%. This describes the collected target distribution; we have not measured a classifier’s held-out accuracy in this step.
# 
# The imbalance supports reporting macro-F1 and per-class results during later evaluation. We will assess class weighting and other training decisions using the appropriate development partitions. We retained the small classes rather than automatically removing or relabeling their records.
# 
# These counts describe our source collection and do not establish exercise prevalence or independently verified muscle targeting.
# 
# #### 3. Description Availability and Text Length
# 
# We found description text for 1,359 exercises. The remaining 1,550 records, representing 53.28% of the catalog, contained name-only classifier inputs.
# 
# Description coverage varied across labels:
# 
# - All eight neck records lacked descriptions.
# - Quadriceps lacked descriptions for 401 of 645 records, or 62.17%.
# - Glutes lacked descriptions for 52 of 81 records, or 64.20%.
# - Traps lacked descriptions for seven of 24 records, or 29.17%.
# 
# Missing text therefore affects classes unevenly and may influence later classification results.
# 
# | Text Population | Records | Mean Words | Median Words | 95th Percentile | Maximum |
# |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
# | Exercise names | 2,909 | 3.86 | 4 | 7 | 11 |
# | Descriptions, including missing values | 2,909 | 25.45 | 0 | 77 | 108 |
# | Available descriptions only | 1,359 | 54.48 | 54 | 85 | 108 |
# | Constructed classifier text | 2,909 | 29.31 | 7 | 81 | 113 |
# 
# The zero median for descriptions across all records reflects missing text. Among records with descriptions, the median was 54 words.
# 
# These results show why we need to examine name-only and described records separately during modeling. The word counts provide descriptive context; the eventual model tokenizer will determine sequence lengths.
# 
# We preserved missing descriptions and continued to construct classifier text from prepared names and available descriptions only.
# 
# #### 4. Linked Groups and Exercise Review Status
# 
# Our analysis reproduced the saved Step 10 grouping structure.
# 
# | Grouping Measure | Result |
# |---|---:|
# | Total linked split groups | 2,581 |
# | Multi-record groups | 192 |
# | Records in multi-record groups | 520 |
# | Mixed-label groups | 46 |
# 
# The 520 records in multi-record groups represent 17.88% of the catalog. Related records therefore form a meaningful part of the data that needs to remain together during later partitioning.
# 
# Per-label group support was lower than record counts in several categories. For example, the 660 abdominals records represented 604 linked groups, while the 645 quadriceps records represented 586 groups.
# 
# These groups provide conservative split protection. They do not establish canonical exercise identity or prove statistical independence. Mixed-label groups can appear under more than one label, so per-label group counts should not be summed as independent groups.
# 
# The review queue contained 2,333 active unresolved entries affecting 2,072 distinct exercise records. Multiple findings can apply to the same exercise.
# 
# EDA preserved these review states. It did not automatically approve records, resolve label disagreements, or exclude every flagged exercise.
# 
# #### 5. Descriptive Categorical Associations
# 
# We examined relationships between source labels, text availability, and exercise metadata using bias-corrected Cramér’s V.
# 
# | Relationship | Cramér’s V | Expected Cells Below Five |
# |---|---:|---:|
# | Body-part label and description availability | 0.1492 | 5.88% |
# | Body-part label and equipment | 0.1982 | 57.92% |
# | Body-part label and difficulty | 0.2172 | 43.14% |
# | Body-part label and exercise type | 0.1945 | 68.07% |
# | Equipment and difficulty | 0.2239 | 38.46% |
# | Exercise type and equipment | 0.2893 | 63.74% |
# 
# Several comparisons contained substantial expected-cell sparsity. We treated the coefficients as descriptions of this catalog and did not assign statistical-significance labels.
# 
# The relationships provide context for uneven documentation and collection patterns. They do not establish causation, classifier performance, or a reason to add these metadata fields to the current text-feature contract.
# 
# #### 6. Food-Reference Completeness and Anomalies
# 
# We examined 411 food-reference records.
# 
# Of these, 321 contained finite values for all four core fields: recorded energy, protein, fat, and carbohydrate. Ninety records lacked a complete finite core.
# 
# After additionally requiring nonnegative core values, 313 records remained. Eight records contained negative carbohydrate measurements.
# 
# | Recorded Field | Missing Values | Negative Values | Finite Nonnegative Values |
# |---|---:|---:|---:|
# | Protein | 12 | 0 | 399 |
# | Fat | 23 | 0 | 388 |
# | Carbohydrate | 59 | 8 | 344 |
# | Fiber | 204 | 0 | 207 |
# | General energy | 89 | 0 | 322 |
# | Specific energy | 99 | 0 | 312 |
# | Selected energy | 89 | 0 | 322 |
# 
# No invalid numerical text or nonfinite measurements were detected in these fields.
# 
# Fiber had the largest missing-value count: 204 records, or 49.64% of the food reference.
# 
# We retained the source values and excluded unsuitable measurements only from the relevant numerical analysis views. We did not replace negative values with zero or infer missing nutrients.
# 
# Fieldwise summaries used different eligible samples. Their means and ranges describe the selected reference records and require confirmation of food identity and reference amount before serving calculations.
# 
# #### 7. Energy Conventions and Food-Composition Relationships
# 
# We checked the selected energy value against its recorded method for 322 records and found no inconsistencies among those checkable records. Another 89 records were uncheckable.
# 
# Across 312 paired records, general energy exceeded specific energy by an average signed difference of 5.688 kcal. The mean absolute difference was 8.977 kcal. Selected energy matched specific energy in their 312 eligible paired records.
# 
# These results support retaining the energy-method information. Differences between conventions should not automatically be treated as errors.
# 
# Our correlations with selected energy were:
# 
# | Nutrient | Eligible Pairs | Pearson Correlation | Spearman Correlation |
# |---|---:|---:|---:|
# | Protein | 321 | 0.5797 | 0.7046 |
# | Fat | 321 | 0.7981 | 0.8020 |
# | Carbohydrate | 313 | 0.4824 | 0.4101 |
# | Fiber | 161 | 0.5458 | 0.7029 |
# 
# The pairwise sample sizes differed because each comparison required both relevant values to be finite and nonnegative. For example, fiber had 207 usable fieldwise values but only 161 eligible fiber–energy pairs.
# 
# These associations describe food composition and can partly reflect how energy is calculated or selected. They do not establish dietary benefit, individual nutritional requirements, or athletic performance effects.
# 
# #### 8. Fictional Profile Coverage and Missing Planning Inputs
# 
# We examined the two fictional profile sources separately.
# 
# | Profile Source | Records | Unknown Injury States | Explicitly Reported No Injury |
# |---|---:|---:|---:|
# | Earlier fictional examples | 30 | 10 | 0 |
# | Development scenarios | 60 | 23 | 35 |
# 
# The development scenarios contained 12 profiles at each age from 13 through 17. This balanced distribution reflects example design rather than population prevalence.
# 
# Both sources lacked five planned input groups:
# 
# - School grade.
# - Session length.
# - Existing training or other commitments.
# - Sleep information.
# - Annual participation.
# 
# Available days alone therefore cannot establish total workload or all scheduling constraints.
# 
# We preserved explicit uncertainty separately from missing information and reported absence of injury. A fictional report of no injury does not establish medical clearance.
# 
# These profiles support development and coverage checks. Their independence as a final test set has not been established, and they do not provide observed training outcomes.
# 
# #### 9. Basketball, Recovery, and Master-Index Coverage
# 
# The imported basketball source contained 23 activity occurrences, 22 normalized name groups, and 23 linked RAG documents.
# 
# Shooting and ball handling each accounted for six occurrences. Other recorded categories included team concepts, passing, offense, footwork and conditioning, warm-up, and defense.
# 
# The derived documents represent the imported activities in another format. They do not add independent drills or coaching evidence.
# 
# Our historical snapshots contained 67 candidate groups in v0.1, 129 candidate groups in v0.2, and 32 local candidate items. These counts overlap and use different record definitions, so we did not combine them.
# 
# Name comparison identified five overlaps with v0.1 and seven with v0.2. The local candidate-item schema did not support the same comparison; its result remained unavailable rather than being reported as zero.
# 
# We also examined five recovery-guidance records and 17 structured reference rules. The carried-forward Step 9 checks recorded no invalid bounds, invalid segment pairs, or incomplete required source fields. This analysis activated no planner rules; applicability remains part of Step 13.
# 
# The master index contained 256,337 heterogeneous records. We retained its role as a source index rather than treating its rows as interchangeable athlete examples or a jointly labeled training dataset.
# 
# #### 10. Reporting Status and Implications
# 
# We prepared 48 evidence tables and nine charts. The integrated reporting included 12 findings and proposed handling for 33 earlier quality findings.
# 
# We kept possible causes distinguishable from established evidence. Missing historical inputs and unresolved content questions remain visible rather than being treated as corrected by structural checks.
# 
# The analysis supports several priorities for later work:
# 
# - Preserve linked groups during dataset partitioning.
# - Evaluate class-level results and text-availability subsets.
# - Investigate label disagreements without automatic reassignment.
# - Apply explicit numerical eligibility checks to food-reference comparisons.
# - Complete the planning-input requirements and applicability review.
# - Keep fictional development examples separate from independently established final evaluation data.
