# ### Data Summary (Step 11)
# 
# #### Dataset Overview and Variables
# 
# Champions Athletic Map uses complementary datasets for supervised exercise classification, basketball coaching retrieval, personalized planning, nutrition reference, and youth participation and recovery constraints. Each source serves a different purpose, so we analyze its records within the appropriate domain.
# 
# Our primary supervised dataset contains **2,909 exercise records across 17 source body-part categories**. The original ten columns include exercise names, available descriptions, body-part labels, exercise types, equipment, difficulty, and other source metadata. Our prepared catalog retains these values and adds fields for text preparation, source identity, grouping, and review. Prepared names and available descriptions form the classifier text; the body-part category remains the target. Equipment, difficulty, identifiers, review fields, goal tags, and existing agent text remain outside the constructed classifier input.
# 
# For basketball coaching, our imported data contains **23 activity occurrences, 22 normalized name groups, and 23 corresponding RAG documents**. The records describe activity names, skill categories, curriculum levels, equipment, participant arrangements, prescriptions, and source references. The documents are derived representations of those activities rather than additional independent drills or coaching sources.
# 
# Step 11 also examined historical basketball snapshots containing **67 candidate groups in v0.1, 129 candidate groups in v0.2, and 32 local candidate items**. These snapshots overlap and use different record definitions. We kept their counts separate rather than treating them as a combined total of distinct approved drills. Their inclusion provides historical review context and does not replace the separately maintained Step 12 catalog and approval records.
# 
# Our nutrition component uses **411 prepared USDA Foundation Foods records**. These contain food identifiers and descriptions, protein, fat, carbohydrate, fiber, alternative energy estimates, and the selected energy value with its recorded method. They support food-reference matching and composition comparisons, subject to checks on identity, measurement units, and reference amounts.
# 
# For planning development, we examined **60 fictional basketball scenarios and 30 earlier fictional athlete examples as separate sources**. Their variables include age, playing position, experience, goals, available days, equipment, and reported injury state. The 60-scenario source also includes scenario labels and optional request annotations. These are development examples, not observed athlete outcomes or an independently established final test set.
# 
# Five recovery-guidance records and seventeen structured reference rules provide information for reviewing participation, rest, sleep, and scheduling constraints. Their fields identify topics or metrics, population segments, rule types, conditional bounds, units, and evidence references.
# 
# A prepared master index contains **256,337 heterogeneous source records**. It supports indexing and lineage across domains; it is not a joined athlete-training matrix. We do not treat the combined counts across these datasets as one machine learning sample or assume that different sources have validated record-level relationships.
# 
# #### Data Quality and Preparation
# 
# The exercise data exhibits substantial class imbalance. Abdominals contains 660 records and quadriceps contains 645; together they represent **44.86%** of the catalog. Neck contains eight records, giving a largest-to-smallest class ratio of **82.5:1**. This uneven coverage may affect recognition of underrepresented categories.
# 
# Descriptions are missing from **1,550 records (53.28%)**, leaving 1,359 records with description text. Missingness also varies by label: all eight neck records lack descriptions, compared with 401 of 645 quadriceps records and seven of 24 traps records. We also retained the earlier finding of 32 records with missing equipment information.
# 
# Our preparation retained all source records, original labels, and source identifiers. We created separate prepared fields and constructed classifier text only from the allowed name and description fields. No formatting changes were required under the implemented preparation rules. Missing descriptions were preserved, and records with usable names and labels remained preliminary classification candidates.
# 
# The expanded exercise review queue contains **2,333 active unresolved entries affecting 2,072 distinct records**. Findings can overlap within a record, so queue entries are not an independent exercise count. These findings identify review needs without automatically establishing that the records are unusable or that their source labels are incorrect.
# 
# We carried forward **2,581 linked split groups**, including 192 multi-record groups covering 520 exercises and 46 mixed-label groups. These groups connect related names, repeated nonempty descriptions, or identical normalized classifier input. They provide constraints for later partitioning rather than canonical identity decisions. We retained separate records and did not automatically resolve label disagreements.
# 
# The food reference also contains incomplete and unusual measurements. **321 records (78.10%)** have finite values for all four core fields: selected energy, protein, fat, and carbohydrate. Ninety records lack a complete finite core. Requiring those core values to be nonnegative leaves **313 records**. Eight records contain negative carbohydrate measurements, while no invalid numerical text or nonfinite measurements were detected in the analyzed nutrient fields.
# 
# Fiber is missing from **204 records (49.64%)**. Protein, fat, carbohydrate, and selected energy have 12, 23, 59, and 89 missing values, respectively. We preserved source values and applied explicit eligibility rules only within the numerical analysis views. We did not fill missing nutrients or replace negative measurements with zero.
# 
# The fictional profile sources contain explicit uncertainty as well as optional annotations. Injury state is unknown in ten of the 30 earlier examples and 23 of the 60 development scenarios. Both schemas lack five planned input groups: school grade, session length, existing commitments, sleep information, and annual participation. We distinguished absent fields, missing values, explicit unknowns, and reported absence of injury. A reported absence of injury does not establish clearance for an activity.
# 
# Nine historical input files remained unavailable in the checked locations. Consequently, we cannot yet attribute every missing or unusual value to the publisher data or an earlier transformation. Source checksums establish consistency with the inspected local versions, while original-source lineage, content reliability, and permissions remain separate questions.
# 
# In a production pipeline, we would address these issues through schema validation, source versioning, integrity checks, controlled preparation, linked-record screening, quality monitoring, and documented review. Learned preprocessing and balancing procedures would use training data only. Regularization and class weighting would be evaluated during model development rather than treated as corrections for uncertain labels or provenance.
# 
# #### Variable Relationships and Exploratory Findings
# 
# Our exercise EDA examined source labels, description availability, equipment, difficulty, exercise type, and text length. Bias-corrected Cramér's V was **0.1492** for body-part label and description availability, **0.1982** for label and equipment, **0.2172** for label and difficulty, and **0.1945** for label and exercise type. Several metadata comparisons had substantial expected-cell sparsity. We treated these coefficients as descriptive relationships within the collected catalog, without significance or causal claims.
# 
# Exercise names averaged **3.86 words**. The 1,359 available descriptions averaged **54.48 words**, with a median of 54 and a maximum of 108. Constructed classifier text averaged 29.31 words, with a median of seven. These results reflect the mixture of name-only and described records and support examining performance by text availability. The counts describe word patterns rather than model-tokenizer lengths.
# 
# The largest source class accounts for **22.69%** of preliminary labeled candidates. This is a full-catalog majority reference, not a measured model accuracy or held-out baseline result.
# 
# For nutrition, we calculated Pearson and Spearman correlations using pairs where both relevant measurements were finite and nonnegative. Each comparison retained its own eligible sample rather than requiring unrelated nutrient fields to be complete.
# 
# | Variable Relationship | Eligible Pairs | Pearson Correlation | Spearman Correlation |
# |---|---:|---:|---:|
# | Fat and selected energy | 321 | 0.7981 | 0.8020 |
# | Protein and selected energy | 321 | 0.5797 | 0.7046 |
# | Carbohydrate and selected energy | 313 | 0.4824 | 0.4101 |
# | Fiber and selected energy | 161 | 0.5458 | 0.7029 |
# 
# The differing denominators matter. For example, fiber had 207 eligible fieldwise values but only 161 eligible pairs with selected energy. We reported coefficients without p-values and used a minimum reporting threshold of ten pairs, which does not by itself establish statistical reliability.
# 
# Our paired energy comparisons also showed recorded measurement dependencies. Selected energy matched specific-factor energy across 312 eligible paired records. General-factor energy exceeded specific-factor energy by an average signed difference of **5.688 kcal**, with a mean absolute difference of **8.977 kcal**. Selected-energy method checks found no inconsistencies among 322 checkable records; 89 records were uncheckable.
# 
# These relationships reflect food composition and the recorded energy-selection convention. They are not independent athlete outcomes or evidence of dietary benefit. Alternative and selected energy fields could provide redundant information or introduce target leakage if used together under an unsuitable predictive objective.
# 
# The imported basketball subset contained six shooting occurrences and six ball-handling occurrences, alongside smaller counts in other recorded categories. We identified five normalized-name overlaps with historical catalog v0.1 and seven with v0.2. The local candidate-item schema did not support the same name comparison, so its result remained unavailable rather than being reported as zero. Name correspondence alone did not resolve activity identity.
# 
# The 60 development scenarios included twelve profiles at each age from thirteen through seventeen. Their balanced age distribution and other relationships describe the example-generation process. They cannot establish real-world athlete prevalence or behavior.
# 
# Recovery records describe guidance and reference constraints rather than measured recovery outcomes. The carried-forward structural checks recorded no invalid bounds, invalid segment pairs, or incomplete required source fields. This analysis activated no planner rules and did not establish individual applicability.
# 
# #### Implications for Machine Learning and Application Development
# 
# For exercise classification, the class imbalance supports reporting **macro-F1, per-class precision, and per-class recall**, alongside accuracy. We will evaluate class weighting and regularization using development results and assess the support available within smaller classes.
# 
# The high proportion of missing descriptions supports retaining usable name-only records and examining performance separately by description availability. All models will use the same name-and-available-description input contract and frozen partitions. We will not generate replacement descriptions.
# 
# We will use the saved linked groups during dataset partitioning to reduce overlap between related training and evaluation records. Group counts are lower than record counts in several classes, and mixed-label groups require additional label review. We will document this pre-split exploration, freeze the evaluation protocol, fit learned transformations using training data, and keep final test performance outside model selection.
# 
# For basketball retrieval and planning, reviewed skill categories, equipment, participant arrangements, source prescriptions, and athlete constraints can support structured filtering. Candidate records will require applicable source, scope, identity, permission, and suitability reviews before planner use. Identifier correspondence between activities and RAG documents does not replace retrieval-quality evaluation.
# 
# For nutrition references, we will use explicit completeness and numerical eligibility checks, confirm food identity and reference amounts, and account for energy-field dependencies before implementing numerical comparisons or serving conversions. Missing measurements will remain distinguishable from valid zero values.
# 
# The fictional profiles will support development and scenario testing. We will extend planning-input coverage according to the requirements established in Step 13 and create a separately reserved final evaluation set. Existing development examples will not be presented as independently validated final-test data.
# 
# Recovery references will inform the development of scheduling checks after reviewing population segments, age and grade applicability, units, and conditional rules. General participation guidance will remain distinguishable from individualized medical or training decisions.
# 
# #### Limitations and Conclusion
# 
# Our EDA provides a clearer account of the available variables and the decisions they can support. However, substantial limitations remain: nine historical inputs are unavailable locally; exercise descriptions are unevenly documented; some source labels have few examples; related exercises and label disagreements require review; food records contain incomplete or unusual measurements; and basketball candidates require further source and approval work.
# 
# The fictional profiles contain no observed training outcomes and omit several planned constraint groups. Recovery references likewise do not provide measured physiological outcomes. None of these sources supplies a validated mapping from an individual youth profile to an effective training plan.
# 
# We prepared **48 evidence tables and nine charts**, including a plain-language variable dictionary, linked-group and review reports, numerical screening results, and integrated findings. The analysis recorded twelve findings and proposed handling for 33 earlier quality findings. Final saving and consistency verification completed successfully: all 66 input and consistency checks passed, and source bytes remained unchanged. The content-review findings described above remain open.
# 
# The collected data provides a practical basis for developing an exercise classifier and a source-linked planning prototype. Demonstrating their quality will require later model and retrieval evaluation, explicit planning constraints, documented source limitations, and continued review. This exploratory analysis does not establish model performance, approved drill coverage, or personalized training effectiveness.
