# <hr style="border: none; height: 10px; background-color: #FFD700;">

# ## 18. Training-Only Text Preprocessing and Feature Preparation
# 
# ### 18.1 Purpose
# 
# In this step, we will prepare the exercise text for our logistic regression and neural classifiers.
# 
# Both models will receive the exercise name and its available description. They will use different numerical representations of that text: TF-IDF features for logistic regression and token IDs for the neural network.
# 
# Our main goal is to make this preparation reproducible while keeping the training, validation, and test partitions separate. Fixed text rules can be applied consistently across partitions, but vocabulary, word-frequency statistics, and other learned settings must come from training records only.
# 
# This step prepares model inputs. It does not train the classifiers or establish that either model performs well.
# 
# ### 18.2 Verify the Catalog and Frozen Splits
# 
# We will begin with the prepared exercise catalog from Step 10 and the frozen split artifacts from Step 15.
# 
# Before preparing any features, we will verify that:
# 
# - The input files match their recorded versions and hashes.
# - Each catalog identifier is unique.
# - Eligible manifest records match the corresponding catalog records.
# - Group assignments and split membership agree with Step 15.
# - No exercise group crosses the training, validation, and test boundaries.
# - The target labels and ordered class list match the frozen benchmark configuration.
# 
# We will join records by `catalog_id`. We will not assume that two files have the same row order.
# 
# The Step 15 manifest will determine eligibility, partition membership, and the benchmark `target_label`. We will not reconstruct those decisions from the original catalog labels or assume that every original class remains eligible.
# 
# If a check fails, we will resolve the mismatch before continuing. We will not silently remove records, change labels, or create a new split during preprocessing.
# 
# ### 18.3 Keep Input Text Separate from Targets and Metadata
# 
# Step 10 already created `classifier_text` from the prepared exercise name followed by its available description, separated by two newline characters when both are present.
# 
# We will reuse and verify that saved text. The original catalog fields and `classifier_text` will remain unchanged.
# 
# | Information | Use in this step |
# |---|---|
# | Exercise name and available description | Classifier input |
# | Benchmark target label from Step 15 | Prediction target |
# | Catalog identifier, group identifier, and split | Alignment and auditing |
# | Original label and review status | Traceability |
# | Source information | Traceability |
# | Target-bearing `agent_text` and derived `goal_tag` | Excluded from classifier inputs |
# | Separate equipment, difficulty, and exercise-type fields | Excluded from this text-only comparison |
# 
# Naturally occurring anatomy or equipment words in the exercise name and description will remain valid input. For example, we will retain “shoulder” when it appears in the original exercise text.
# 
# Athlete profiles, basketball reference documents, nutrition records, and private evaluation expectations will not become exercise-classifier features.
# 
# The target represents the benchmark’s recorded body-part category. Predicting it does not establish an exercise’s suitability for a particular athlete.
# 
# ### 18.4 Apply a Shared Text Preparation Policy
# 
# We will create a separate `model_text` representation from the saved `classifier_text`.
# 
# Our initial preparation will:
# 
# 1. Apply Unicode NFKC normalization.
# 2. Replace repeated whitespace with a single space.
# 3. Remove leading and trailing whitespace.
# 4. Apply consistent Unicode case folding.
# 
# These operations will not change the protected source text.
# 
# We will begin without stop-word removal, stemming, lemmatization, or generated descriptions. Words such as “not,” movement directions, anatomy terms, and useful numbers may carry information that we want the models to retain.
# 
# A missing description will remain absent. It will not become the literal text `"nan"` or `"None"`, and we will not fill it with an estimated description.
# 
# For tokenization, our initial shared expression will be:
# 
# `r"(?u)\b\w+(?:[.'’/-]\w+)*\b"`
# 
# This keeps word-like tokens and allows internal apostrophes, hyphens, slashes, and decimal points. For example, `one-arm`, `don't`, `1/2`, and `2.5` can remain individual tokens.
# 
# We will check representative examples before freezing this policy. These checks will confirm the intended behavior; they will not use test records to choose a more favorable tokenizer.
# 
# The normalization and tokenization functions will live in an importable project module so training, saved models, and application predictions can use the same implementation.
# 
# ### 18.5 Maintain the Training Boundary
# 
# Each partition will have a defined role.
# 
# | Partition | Permitted use |
# |---|---|
# | Training | Build learned representations and train model parameters |
# | Validation | Apply training-derived preparation and compare development choices |
# | Test | Apply frozen preparation during final evaluation in Step 32 |
# 
# Vocabulary counts, inverse-document-frequency values, neural vocabulary membership, and the sequence-length setting will not use validation or test records.
# 
# Validation results can support the predefined model comparisons in Step 21. They will not add words to a fitted vocabulary or change training-derived statistics.
# 
# During Step 18, we will prepare training and validation inputs. Test identity and integrity checks may be performed, but test tokenization, vocabulary-coverage analysis, truncation diagnostics, feature transformation, and predictions will wait until Step 32.
# 
# Our earlier dataset-wide EDA will remain documented. We will not describe the project as though the source data had never been inspected.
# 
# ### 18.6 Define the TF-IDF Configuration
# 
# The logistic regression classifier will use TF-IDF features for individual tokens and adjacent token pairs.
# 
# We will start with the following fixed configuration.
# 
# | Setting | Initial choice |
# |---|---|
# | Analyzer | Word |
# | Tokenizer | Shared tokenizer from Section 18.4 |
# | Token pattern | `None`, because tokenization is supplied explicitly |
# | Additional lowercasing | Disabled; handled by shared normalization |
# | Accent stripping | None |
# | N-gram range | Unigrams and bigrams: `(1, 2)` |
# | Maximum features | 10,000 |
# | Minimum document frequency | 1 document |
# | Maximum document frequency | 1.0 |
# | Stop words | None |
# | Binary term counts | Disabled |
# | IDF weighting and smoothing | Enabled |
# | Sublinear term-frequency scaling | Disabled |
# | Row normalization | L2 |
# | Numeric representation | Float64 sparse matrix |
# 
# We will supply the custom function as the tokenizer while keeping the word analyzer. This preserves the configured bigram construction.
# 
# Step 18 will save this configuration. **Step 19 will fit the complete preprocessing and logistic regression pipeline on training records.** This gives us one clear owner for the fitted TF-IDF vocabulary and IDF values.
# 
# Validation inputs will use that fitted pipeline. We will keep the feature matrices sparse and record their dimensions and nonzero counts in the model-training step.
# 
# TF-IDF’s L2 row normalization and logistic regression’s L2 regularization serve different purposes. We will record them separately.
# 
# The majority-class baseline will use training labels and will not require TF-IDF features.
# 
# ### 18.7 Build the Neural Vocabulary from Training Text
# 
# The neural classifier will represent each token with an integer ID.
# 
# We will reserve two separate IDs:
# 
# | ID | Meaning |
# |---:|---|
# | 0 | `PAD`: a position added for batching |
# | 1 | `UNK`: a token outside the retained training vocabulary |
# 
# Ordinary vocabulary tokens will begin at ID 2.
# 
# Our initial vocabulary policy will:
# 
# - Count tokens across the full, untruncated training inputs.
# - Retain tokens appearing at least twice in training.
# - Keep at most 10,000 ordinary tokens.
# - Rank tokens by descending training frequency.
# - Resolve frequency ties using a fixed lexical order.
# 
# The maximum vocabulary size will therefore be 10,002 entries, including `PAD` and `UNK`. The saved artifact will report the actual size.
# 
# The frequency threshold refers to total token occurrences, rather than the number of documents containing a token.
# 
# Rare training tokens, tokens beyond the vocabulary cap, and unseen validation tokens will map to `UNK`. Validation and test records will never expand the vocabulary.
# 
# In Step 20, we will train the embedding weights from scratch. The padding row will remain zero and be excluded from pooling. We will initialize the unknown-token row to zero and allow it to learn when unknown-token positions occur in training.
# 
# We will report whether `UNK` actually appears in the training sequences after truncation. If it does not, we will document that its embedding has not learned a fallback representation.
# 
# ### 18.8 Set Sequence Length, Truncation, and Padding
# 
# We will choose the neural sequence-length limit from training token lengths only.
# 
# The initial rule will be:
# 
# **Maximum sequence length = the training 95th-percentile token length, bounded between 16 and 256 tokens.**
# 
# We will calculate the percentile using the nearest-rank method and save the resulting integer. Token lengths will be measured before truncation, after applying the empty-token handling described below.
# 
# The exercise name already appears first in the input. We will retain the first tokens up to the saved limit and truncate the remainder on the right.
# 
# This keeps the beginning of the text, but it may remove information from long descriptions. We will report how often truncation occurs and how many tokens it removes.
# 
# Within each batch, sequences will be padded on the right to the longest retained sequence in that batch, without exceeding the saved limit.
# 
# The neural inputs will include:
# 
# - Integer token IDs.
# - A Boolean mask identifying non-padding positions.
# - Retained sequence lengths.
# - Integer target class IDs.
# 
# For masked mean pooling, padding must be excluded from both the embedding sum and the token count. `UNK` represents an input position and must remain in the count.
# 
# An all-padding sequence will be treated as an implementation or input-contract error.
# 
# The sequence-length rule and vocabulary will remain fixed during the initial model comparisons in Steps 20 and 21.
# 
# ### 18.9 Preserve Labels and Handle Difficult Inputs Consistently
# 
# We will copy the ordered class list and label-to-index mapping from Step 15.
# 
# We will verify that every eligible target maps to one class index and maps back to the same label. An unexpected target will stop preparation for investigation.
# 
# Token IDs and target class IDs belong to different mappings. In particular, target class ID 0 is a valid class and must not be ignored because padding also uses token ID 0.
# 
# If class weights are used later, they will be calculated from training labels only. We will not resample validation or test records.
# 
# Difficult inputs will follow predefined handling rules.
# 
# | Situation | Required handling |
# |---|---|
# | Missing description with a valid exercise name | Retain the name-only input |
# | Genuinely empty canonical input | Stop and reconcile the data-contract problem |
# | Valid nonempty text producing no tokens | Use one `UNK` position for the neural model and flag the record |
# | Valid neural input containing only unknown tokens | Retain it and record the condition |
# | Valid all-zero TF-IDF row | Keep the fitted pipeline’s normal prediction and retain the record in evaluation |
# | Unexpected label, identifier, or split mismatch | Stop and investigate |
# 
# We will not remove difficult examples, extend a vocabulary using held-out text, or substitute a different prediction rule to improve reported scores.
# 
# ### 18.10 Check Reproducibility and Keep the Comparison Fair
# 
# Before training, our checks will confirm that:
# 
# - Training and validation record counts match the frozen manifest.
# - Text, targets, and identifiers remain aligned.
# - Only permitted text reaches the feature preparation functions.
# - Repeated preparation produces the same normalized text and token IDs.
# - Vocabulary IDs are unique and within the recorded range.
# - Padding and unknown tokens have different IDs.
# - Sequence lengths, masks, and truncation follow the saved policy.
# - Label encoding preserves the frozen class order.
# - Every eligible training and validation record remains accounted for.
# 
# We will report training and validation token lengths, unknown-token rates, truncation counts, and the number of name-only inputs. Where useful, these diagnostics will also be summarized by class.
# 
# TF-IDF feature dimensions and zero-vector checks will be added when the pipeline is fitted in Step 19.
# 
# Both classifiers will receive the same underlying exercise text and benchmark records. Their representations will still differ: TF-IDF uses the full prepared text, while the neural model applies the documented sequence limit.
# 
# We will acknowledge this difference when interpreting results. A performance gap cannot automatically be attributed to network architecture alone.
# 
# ### 18.11 Expected Outputs and Handoff
# 
# After completing this step, we expect to save:
# 
# | Output | Purpose |
# |---|---|
# | `src/champion_athletic_map/text_preprocessing.py` | Shared normalization, tokenization, and neural input preparation |
# | `data/processed/exercise_model_inputs.csv` | Training and validation text, identifiers, targets, and class indices |
# | `artifacts/preprocessing/preprocessing_config.json` | Fixed text rules, TF-IDF settings, vocabulary policy, and actual sequence limit |
# | `artifacts/preprocessing/label_mapping.json` | Frozen class mapping inherited from Step 15 |
# | `artifacts/preprocessing/neural_vocabulary.json` | Training-derived token mapping, retained frequencies, and reserved IDs |
# | `results/preprocessing_diagnostics.csv` | Training and validation coverage, length, and truncation summaries |
# | `results/preprocessing_checks.csv` | Input, alignment, mapping, and reproducibility checks |
# | `results/step18_preprocessing_summary.json` | Producer record linking inputs, settings, checks, and saved outputs |
# 
# The model-input table may contain targets for alignment, but training code will select the text and target columns explicitly. Targets and audit fields will never be concatenated into the input.
# 
# The producer record will include input and output hashes, the ordered training identifiers, the split-manifest version, software versions, and the actual vocabulary and sequence dimensions.
# 
# Step 19 will fit the TF-IDF baseline pipeline. Step 20 will train the neural classifier using the saved vocabulary and sequence policy. Step 22 will package the selected model with its exact preprocessing dependencies.
# 
# Passing this step will establish that the development inputs are prepared consistently. Model performance and final test results will be reported in the later evaluation steps.
# 
# 

# In[28]:


# Step 18-1 — Verify frozen inputs and retain training/validation exercise text.
# Read-only: no normalization, tokenization, learned features, model runs or saves.
import csv
import hashlib
import io
import json
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
from pathlib import Path

import pandas as pd
from IPython.display import display

step18_inputs = None
step18_status = {"stage": "checking_prerequisites", "check": "kernel_receipt", "features_prepared": False}


def inspect_step18_inputs(protocol_receipt):
    root = Path(protocol_receipt["root"]).resolve()
    hashes, checks = {}, []
    def check(name, condition, observed="verified"):
        step18_status["check"] = name
        if not bool(condition):
            raise RuntimeError("step18_input_check_failed")
        checks.append({"check": name, "status": "pass", "observed": str(observed)})
    def path(relative):
        check_path = root / relative
        if (not isinstance(relative, str) or Path(relative).is_absolute() or "\\" in relative
                or check_path != check_path.resolve() or not check_path.is_relative_to(root) or check_path == root):
            raise RuntimeError("invalid_project_path")
        return check_path
    def sha(content):
        return hashlib.sha256(content).hexdigest()
    def packed(value):
        return (json.dumps(value, ensure_ascii=False, sort_keys=True, indent=2, allow_nan=False) + "\n").encode()
    def verify(relative, expected):
        digest = hashlib.sha256()
        with path(relative).open("rb") as handle:
            for chunk in iter(lambda: handle.read(1024 * 1024), b""):
                digest.update(chunk)
        observed = digest.hexdigest()
        if observed != expected or (relative in hashes and hashes[relative] != observed):
            raise RuntimeError("input_identity_changed")
        hashes[relative] = observed
    def contents(relative):
        if relative not in hashes:
            raise RuntimeError("unverified_input")
        content = path(relative).read_bytes()
        if sha(content) != hashes[relative]:
            raise RuntimeError("input_changed_during_read")
        return content
    def read_json(relative):
        return json.loads(contents(relative))
    def metadata(relative, columns, spec=None):
        content = contents(relative)
        header = pd.read_csv(io.BytesIO(content), nrows=0, dtype=str, keep_default_na=False, encoding="utf-8-sig").columns.tolist()
        if spec is not None:
            check("saved_schema:" + Path(relative).stem, header == spec["columns"])
        check("required_metadata:" + Path(relative).stem, set(columns).issubset(header))
        frame = pd.read_csv(io.BytesIO(content), usecols=columns, dtype=str, keep_default_na=False, encoding="utf-8-sig")
        if spec is not None:
            check("saved_rows:" + Path(relative).stem, len(frame) == spec["rows"], len(frame))
        return frame[columns]

    step18_status["stage"] = "verifying_project_and_protocol_handoff"
    executable = shutil.which("git")
    if executable is None and os.name == "nt":
        choices = [Path(base) / "Git/cmd/git.exe" for name in ["ProgramFiles", "ProgramFiles(x86)"] if (base := os.environ.get(name))]
        if os.environ.get("LOCALAPPDATA"):
            choices.append(Path(os.environ["LOCALAPPDATA"]) / "Programs/Git/cmd/git.exe")
        executable = next((str(value) for value in choices if value.is_file()), None)
    check("git_available", executable is not None)
    def git(*arguments):
        result = subprocess.run([executable, "-C", str(root), *arguments], capture_output=True, check=False)
        if result.returncode:
            raise RuntimeError("git_boundary_unavailable")
        return result.stdout.decode().strip()
    check("project_and_branch", Path(git("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")).resolve() == root
        and git("branch", "--show-current") == "sam-development")
    summary_path = "results/step17_evaluation_design_summary.json"
    config_path = "artifacts/evaluation/scoring_config.json"
    check("protocol_preparation_receipt", protocol_receipt["status"] == "protocol_preparation_complete_final_scoring_blocked"
        and protocol_receipt["definitions_prepared"] is True
        and protocol_receipt["classifier_feature_preparation_can_continue"] is True)
    for relative in [summary_path, config_path]:
        verify(relative, protocol_receipt["artifact_hashes"][relative])
    protocol = read_json(summary_path)
    identity = {"recipe_version": protocol["recipe_version"], "input_hashes": protocol["input_hashes"], "artifact_hashes": protocol["artifacts"]}
    check("saved_protocol_identity", sha(packed(identity)) == protocol["protocol_digest"] == protocol_receipt["protocol_digest"]
        and protocol["input_hashes"] == protocol_receipt["input_hashes"]
        and protocol["artifacts"][config_path] == hashes[config_path])
    scoring_config = read_json(config_path)
    check("classifier_preparation_handoff", protocol["definitions_prepared"] is True
        and protocol["classifier_feature_preparation_can_continue"] is True
        and protocol["protocol_ready"] is scoring_config["protocol_ready"] is False
        and protocol["final_classifier_scoring_ready"] is scoring_config["final_classifier_scoring_ready"] is False)
    # Only these two definition files are classifier handoff dependencies here.
    # This inspection does not reopen profile files or human-review tables.

    step18_status["stage"] = "verifying_frozen_exercise_input_bytes"
    producer_path = "results/step15_exercise_split_summary.json"
    verify(producer_path, protocol["input_hashes"][producer_path])
    producer = read_json(producer_path)
    outputs = {"data/processed/exercise_groups.csv", "data/processed/exercise_split_manifest.csv", "results/exercise_benchmark_review_decisions.csv",
        "results/exercise_class_eligibility.csv", "results/exercise_split_candidates.csv", "results/exercise_split_summary.csv",
        "results/exercise_split_checks.csv", "artifacts/splits/exercise_split_config.json"}
    check("frozen_benchmark_record", producer["schema_version"] == "step15-exercise-split-1"
        and producer["status"] == "frozen" and producer["benchmark_frozen"] is True and set(producer["artifacts"]) == outputs)
    for relative, expected in producer["input_hashes"].items():
        verify(relative, expected)
    output_hashes = {relative: spec["sha256"] for relative, spec in producer["artifacts"].items()}
    for relative, expected in output_hashes.items():
        verify(relative, expected)
    frozen_identity = json.dumps({"inputs": producer["input_hashes"], "outputs": output_hashes}, ensure_ascii=False, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":")).encode()
    check("frozen_benchmark_identity", "exercise-benchmark:" + sha(frozen_identity) == producer["benchmark_id"]
        == scoring_config["benchmark"]["benchmark_id"])
    split_config = read_json("artifacts/splits/exercise_split_config.json")
    preparation = read_json("results/step10_exercise_preparation.json")
    classes = producer["class_order"]
    mapping = {label: index for index, label in enumerate(classes)}
    contract = producer["feature_contract"]
    check("class_mapping_and_feature_contract", classes == sorted(set(classes)) == split_config["class_order"]
        == scoring_config["benchmark"]["class_order"] and len(classes) >= 2
        and mapping == producer["class_to_index"] == split_config["class_to_index"] == scoring_config["benchmark"]["class_to_index"]
        and contract == split_config["feature_contract"]
        and contract["input_fields"] == preparation["feature_contract"]["input_fields"] == ["exercise_name_prepared", "description_prepared"]
        and contract["model_text_field"] == "classifier_text" and contract["target_field"] == "target_label"
        and contract["source_target_field"] == "body_part_prepared" and contract["split_group_field"] == "split_group_id"
        and contract["final_splits_created"] is True and split_config["selection_uses_model_scores"] is False
        and split_config["input_hashes"] == producer["input_hashes"] and split_config["selected_allocation"] == producer["selected_candidate"])

    step18_status["stage"] = "loading_frozen_assignment_metadata"
    manifest_path = "data/processed/exercise_split_manifest.csv"
    manifest_columns = producer["artifacts"][manifest_path]["columns"]
    manifest = metadata(manifest_path, manifest_columns, producer["artifacts"][manifest_path])
    check("manifest_identifiers", manifest["catalog_id"].is_unique and manifest["catalog_id"].str.strip().ne("").all())
    active = manifest.loc[manifest["benchmark_eligible"].eq("yes")].copy()
    excluded = manifest.loc[manifest["benchmark_eligible"].eq("no")]
    check("eligibility_and_split_assignments", manifest["benchmark_eligible"].isin(["yes", "no"]).all()
        and active["split"].isin(["train", "validation", "test"]).all() and active["exclusion_reason"].eq("").all()
        and excluded["split"].eq("").all() and excluded["class_index"].eq("").all() and excluded["exclusion_reason"].ne("").all())
    check("group_separation_and_class_indices", active["split_group_id"].str.strip().ne("").all()
        and active.groupby("split_group_id")["split"].nunique().eq(1).all()
        and sorted(active["target_label"].unique()) == classes
        and active["class_index"].tolist() == active["target_label"].map(lambda label: str(mapping[label])).tolist())
    population = producer["population"]
    check("frozen_population_counts", len(manifest) == population["source_records"] and len(active) == population["eligible_records"]
        and len(excluded) == population["excluded_records"] and active["split_group_id"].nunique() == population["eligible_groups"]
        and len(classes) == population["eligible_classes"] and excluded["exclusion_reason"].value_counts().to_dict() == population["exclusion_reasons"])
    dev_ids = set(active.loc[active["split"].isin(["train", "validation"]), "catalog_id"])

    step18_status["stage"] = "streaming_catalog_and_retaining_development_text"
    catalog_path = "data/processed/exercise_catalog.csv"
    catalog_spec = preparation["artifacts"]["catalog"]
    check("catalog_producer_binding", catalog_spec["relative_path"] == catalog_path and catalog_spec["sha256"] == hashes[catalog_path])
    metadata_columns = ["catalog_id", "source_file", "source_sha256", "source_cleaned_record_position", "split_group_id",
        "preparation_version", "label_review_status", "review_flags", "text_availability", "body_part_prepared"]
    text_columns = ["catalog_id", "exercise_name_prepared", "description_prepared", "classifier_text"]
    catalog_rows, development_rows = [], []
    # csv.reader handles quoted multiline records. Each row is transiently decoded;
    # only declared train/validation rows have text fields selected and retained.
    with path(catalog_path).open("r", encoding="utf-8-sig", newline="") as handle:
        reader = csv.reader(handle, strict=True)
        header = next(reader)
        check("catalog_header", header == catalog_spec["columns"] and len(header) == len(set(header))
            and all(isinstance(name, str) and name.strip() for name in header)
            and set(metadata_columns + text_columns).issubset(header))
        positions = {name: header.index(name) for name in set(metadata_columns + text_columns)}
        row_width_valid = True
        for row in reader:
            if len(row) != len(header):
                row_width_valid = False
                break
            facts = {name: row[positions[name]] for name in metadata_columns}
            catalog_rows.append(facts)
            if facts["catalog_id"] in dev_ids:
                development_rows.append({name: row[positions[name]] for name in text_columns})
        check("catalog_logical_row_widths", row_width_valid)
    catalog = pd.DataFrame(catalog_rows, columns=metadata_columns)
    check("catalog_coverage_and_identity", len(catalog) == catalog_spec["rows"] == len(manifest)
        and catalog["catalog_id"].is_unique and set(catalog["catalog_id"]) == set(manifest["catalog_id"])
        and catalog["source_sha256"].eq(preparation["parent_source"]["sha256"]).all()
        and catalog["source_file"].eq(preparation["parent_source"]["relative_path"]).all()
        and catalog["preparation_version"].eq(preparation["recipe_version"]).all())
    aligned = manifest.set_index("catalog_id").sort_index()
    original_metadata = catalog.set_index("catalog_id").sort_index()
    compare_columns = [name for name in metadata_columns if name != "catalog_id"]
    check("metadata_alignment_by_identifier", aligned[compare_columns].equals(original_metadata[compare_columns]))
    source_positions = pd.to_numeric(catalog["source_cleaned_record_position"], errors="raise").tolist()
    check("source_positions", sorted(source_positions) == list(range(1, len(catalog) + 1)))
    groups_path = "data/processed/exercise_groups.csv"
    groups = metadata(groups_path, ["catalog_id", "split_group_id"], producer["artifacts"][groups_path])
    check("authoritative_groups", groups["catalog_id"].is_unique
        and groups.set_index("catalog_id").sort_index()["split_group_id"].equals(aligned["split_group_id"]))
    decisions_path = "results/exercise_benchmark_review_decisions.csv"
    decisions = metadata(decisions_path, ["scope", "catalog_id", "decision_id", "target_label"], producer["artifacts"][decisions_path])
    record_decisions = decisions.loc[decisions["scope"].eq("record")].set_index("catalog_id")
    check("reviewed_target_links", decisions["decision_id"].is_unique and record_decisions.index.is_unique
        and set(record_decisions.index) == set(aligned.index)
        and record_decisions.loc[aligned.index, "decision_id"].tolist() == aligned["review_decision_ref"].tolist()
        and record_decisions.loc[aligned.index, "target_label"].tolist() == aligned["target_label"].tolist())

    step18_status["stage"] = "checking_development_text_and_split_support"
    text = pd.DataFrame(development_rows, columns=text_columns)
    check("development_text_coverage", text["catalog_id"].is_unique and set(text["catalog_id"]) == dev_ids)
    development_metadata = active.loc[active["catalog_id"].isin(dev_ids),
        ["catalog_id", "split_group_id", "split", "target_label", "class_index", "text_availability"]]
    development = development_metadata.merge(text, on="catalog_id", how="inner", validate="one_to_one").sort_values("catalog_id").reset_index(drop=True)
    reconstructed = ["\n\n".join(value for value in (name, description) if value)
        for name, description in zip(development["exercise_name_prepared"], development["description_prepared"])]
    check("saved_canonical_text_preserved", development["exercise_name_prepared"].str.strip().ne("").all()
        and development["classifier_text"].str.strip().ne("").all() and development["classifier_text"].tolist() == reconstructed)
    expected_availability = ["name_and_description" if value else "name_only" for value in development["description_prepared"]]
    check("description_missingness_preserved", development["text_availability"].tolist() == expected_availability)
    development["class_index"] = development["class_index"].astype(int)
    check("label_roundtrip", development["target_label"].tolist() == [classes[index] for index in development["class_index"]])
    support_path = "results/exercise_split_summary.csv"
    support = metadata(support_path, ["scope", "split", "records", "groups", "classes_present"], producer["artifacts"][support_path])
    overall = support.loc[support["scope"].eq("overall")].set_index("split")
    check("saved_split_support_keys", overall.index.is_unique and set(overall.index) == {"train", "validation", "test"})
    class_path = "results/exercise_class_eligibility.csv"
    class_support = metadata(class_path, ["target_label", *[split + suffix for split in ["train", "validation", "test"] for suffix in ["_records", "_groups"]]],
        producer["artifacts"][class_path]).set_index("target_label")
    check("class_support_keys", class_support.index.is_unique and set(classes).issubset(class_support.index))
    split_counts = []
    for split in ["train", "validation", "test"]:
        part = active.loc[active["split"].eq(split)]
        count, distinct_groups = len(part), part["split_group_id"].nunique()
        check("saved_split_support:" + split, count == int(overall.at[split, "records"])
            and distinct_groups == int(overall.at[split, "groups"]) and int(overall.at[split, "classes_present"]) == len(classes), count)
        for label in classes:
            subset = part.loc[part["target_label"].eq(label)]
            if (len(subset) != int(class_support.at[label, split + "_records"])
                    or subset["split_group_id"].nunique() != int(class_support.at[label, split + "_groups"])
                    or subset["split_group_id"].nunique() < split_config["support_minimum_groups"][split]):
                raise RuntimeError("class_support_differs")
        check("saved_class_support:" + split, True)
        split_counts.append({"split": split, "records": count, "groups": distinct_groups,
            "text_retained_for_preparation": split != "test"})
    training = development.loc[development["split"].eq("train")].reset_index(drop=True)
    validation = development.loc[development["split"].eq("validation")].reset_index(drop=True)
    check("development_partition_sizes", len(training) + len(validation) == len(dev_ids)
        and len(training) == int(overall.at["train", "records"]) and len(validation) == int(overall.at["validation", "records"])
        and not set(development["catalog_id"]).intersection(active.loc[active["split"].eq("test"), "catalog_id"]))
    preparation_checks = metadata("results/exercise_split_checks.csv", ["status"], producer["artifacts"]["results/exercise_split_checks.csv"])
