# ### Observation — Step 31
# 
# We tested our four planners on the same 20 development cases using llama3.1:8b. We saved all 80 outcomes. This initial pilot used 247 model requests, including specialist reviews and revisions.
# 
# | Planner | Correctly formatted responses |
# |---|---:|
# | 1 — Language model alone | 10/20 |
# | 2 — Language model with references | 11/20 |
# | 3 — References and specialist roles | 13/20 |
# | 4 — Checks and revisions | 13/20 |
# 
# These counts measure response format only. Our separate checks found missing or changed existing practices, incorrect numbers of sessions, and missing or unverified food answers. No generated training plan was fully verified by all required checks. Some checks also remained unresolved because the evidence or checker coverage was incomplete.
# 
# In the initial pilot, Planner 4 could not finish five cases: three requests exceeded the available input space, and two replies reached their length limit. Its only internally accepted response was a clarification.
# 
# Our later review also found that approved drill records were absent from the original pilot inputs. We added approved drill context where available and corrected parts of the response requirements, request-size handling and food validation.
# 
# We tested these corrections separately and retained the original results. Five saved revision requests passed the backend size checks. A continued Planner 4 example returned a correctly formatted plan, but it still omitted existing practices and activity times, left the food question unanswered, and did not provide verified resource evidence. These follow-up tests therefore did not establish reliable planning.
# 
# At the end of Step 31, we froze the exercise-classifier evaluation settings for the majority baseline, L5 logistic regression and N3 neural classifier with seed 42. This allows us to proceed with the classifier evaluation in Step 32.
# 
# Final planner testing still requires a reviewed final case set, retrieval assessment and reviewer preparation. Final planner scoring and human review remain pending.

# <hr style="border: none; height: 10px; background-color: #FFD700;">

# ### Step 32 — Final Exercise Classifier Evaluation and Error Analysis
# 
# In this step, we test how well our saved classifiers identify an exercise's body-part label from its name and description. This is the exercise-text classification part of our project. These results do not measure the quality of the basketball calendars or show whether an exercise is suitable for a particular athlete.
# 
# We compare the three models selected during development:
# 
# - **Majority baseline:** always predicts the most common training label. This gives us a simple result to compare against.
# - **L5 logistic regression:** uses the saved TF-IDF pipeline to classify the exercise text.
# - **N3 neural classifier:** uses the saved vocabulary and the best validation checkpoint from seed 42. This is our exercise classifier, separate from the language model used by the planners.
# 
# All three models use the same frozen test set: **438 exercise records in 385 groups, covering 16 labels**. Related exercise records stay in the same split. We first check the saved files, model versions, class mapping and group membership, then use each model's original preprocessing. We do not retrain the models, change the vocabulary or select a different checkpoint after seeing these results.
# 
# Our main measure is **macro-F1**, which gives each label equal weight. We also report accuracy, results for each label and confusion matrices showing which labels the models mix up. The saved scores keep their full precision; the displayed table is rounded for readability. Every model must return one valid prediction for every test record before we report the complete comparison.
# 
# We also review errors and input characteristics, including missing descriptions, unfamiliar words, text length and inputs that contain no known TF-IDF features. Valid name-only and unfamiliar-word examples stay in the test set. The error examples follow a fixed selection rule so that we do not choose only convenient cases. We keep the original benchmark labels, including any remaining source-label limitations, and treat additional error analysis as exploratory.
# 
# The cell runs software checks before the evaluation and saves the predictions, scores, diagnostic tables, figures and findings. Completed predictions are reused on a repeat run. If a technical problem interrupts a model, we retain its attempt record rather than silently generating replacement results.
# 
# The test results will not change the application's validation-selected L5 model or feed new tags into the planners. We leave confidence intervals for Step 35 and keep final planner testing and human review separate. The earlier full-source preparation and exploration remain a limitation of this benchmark.
# 

