# ### Step 33-2 — Prepare the Case Corrections
# 
# We will turn our review findings into a separate set of proposed case corrections. Our original 200 drafts will remain available so that we can trace each proposed version back to its original facts. This cell prepares the corrections and supporting evidence for team review before we freeze the final test set.
# 
# For the 150 cases intended to produce plans, we will propose an explicit statement that the listed fictional week is complete. This is a new case-authoring decision, not something we can infer from an empty or nonempty list of commitments. The proposal will record the original and new versions and leave the team decision open. The 50 challenge cases will retain their missing information, conflicting constraints or outside-scope requests.
# 
# We will also address the activity evidence. We screened each planning case against all 136 approved activity records, including 127 basketball records and nine warm-ups. The resulting proposals contain source-linked options, known resource requirements and specific questions still needing review. Where the source gives a suitable elapsed duration and the stated resources support the base activity, we include a calendar example. Repetitions, makes and games will not be converted into invented minutes. These examples support case preparation; they do not approve an activity for an athlete or complete every case-level rule check.
# 
# The food questions need a wording correction as well. Our draft cases use “recorded food identity” and “are listed,” while the current lookup recognizes “For the food” and “are recorded.” We will propose the supported wording while preserving each exact food name, nutrient and quantity. The expected numeric answers will come from the frozen USDA reference and its existing calculation helper. We will record source IDs, quantities, units and qualifications, and keep unavailable or ambiguous results visible. A verified private answer and a working runtime lookup will be recorded separately.
# 
# We will then repeat the saved relationship screen against the full 100-case development pool and the 20 versioned pilot inputs. Those 20 are amended states of selected development cases, not 20 additional independent athletes. The new results will retain the earlier 618 review flags. Similarity alone will not merge families, exclude cases or establish independence.
# 
# The output will include proposed public records, a correction register, activity examples and remaining questions, private food expectations, the updated relationship screen, and editable team-decision files. Public proposals will contain case facts only. Expected answers, activity witnesses, categories, family findings and review decisions will stay in the private evaluation materials.
# 
# We will use the new notes page and working checklist to complete team review. Rerunning this preparation will preserve edits in the working files. Final case acceptance and the planner protocol freeze will follow after the unresolved decisions and required evidence are complete.
# 

