# ## Step 34 — Team review of the saved replies
# 
# We review the replies from Step 33 to understand how useful they are for the requests we gave our planners. A reply can be correctly formatted and still change a practice time, miss a requested food fact, or leave the athlete unsure what to do. We also give credit to useful content that our automatic checks could not fully recognize.
# 
# We use the 40-profile sample selected before the final results were examined. It includes 25 feasible requests, five with missing information, five with conflicting requirements, and five outside our project scope. Every age from 13 to 17 contributes eight profiles. With all four planner versions, we review **160 unchanged replies**.
# 
# We keep the five existing review categories separate:
# 
# | Category | What we check |
# |---|---|
# | Response suitability | Does the reply give the right kind of answer to the actual request? |
# | Profile fit | Does it respect the athlete's schedule, resources and relevant information? |
# | Practicality and completeness | Is it useful enough to follow, and are important parts missing? |
# | Evidence accuracy | Do its factual statements agree with the input and approved references? |
# | Clarity | Is the answer understandable and consistent? |
# 
# Each category uses the same **0, 1 or 2** scale: does not meet expectations, partly meets expectations, or meets expectations. We record a reason and supporting evidence for every rating. Evidence accuracy is left unscored only when there is no substantive claim to check. We assess readable text as received, including replies that failed the response format, and keep an empty returned reply in the sample.
# 
# For this step, we use **one assisted review under Sam's direction**. This changes the original plan for two independently rating teammates. We record the method change and leave reviewer agreement unestimated. The completed review contains **800 category entries**; it is separate from the original two-reviewer forms.
# 
# We check schedule claims against the original athlete information, drill claims against the approved activities, and food statements against our frozen USDA records. A chosen practice duration is not treated as a publisher prescription. Missing details and uncertain resource use remain distinct from a demonstrated contradiction.
# 
# The code below records the completed review, verifies that its replies and references match our saved study, and creates a readable report. It does not generate replacement plans or change the 800 original outcomes or their automatic scores. We keep the five categories separate, without adding an overall quality score or declaring a winning planner. The sample contains more challenge cases than the full comparison, so its ratings describe this selected set.
# 

# In[27]:


