# <hr style="border: none; height: 10px; background-color: #FFD700;">

# ## Step 35 — Statistical comparisons and results analysis
# 
# We bring our saved classifier, retrieval, planner and team-review results together to understand what worked, what failed, and what the additional workflow steps cost in running time. We use the original saved outputs. We do not retrain the classifiers, generate replacement plans, repeat the retrieval test, change the benchmark labels, or adjust a planner after seeing its final results.
# 
# ### What we compare
# 
# Our planner comparison contains **200 fictional profiles and 800 outcomes**. Each profile was given to all four planners, so we compare replies for the same request rather than compare different groups of athletes. The main planning measure keeps all **150 feasible profiles per planner**. The 20 missing-information, 15 conflict and 15 outside-scope cases remain separate.
# 
# We keep response format, workflow completion and complete request fulfillment separate. A finished reply can still contain an invalid format, and a correctly formatted plan can still change an existing practice or miss a required answer. We preserve failures, required unknowns and incomplete checks instead of counting them as successful plans.
# 
# The four planned comparisons are:
# 
# | Comparison | What changes between the workflows |
# |---|---|
# | Planner 2 minus Planner 1 | References and recorded factual lookups |
# | Planner 3 minus Planner 2 | Specialist contributions and coordination |
# | Planner 4 minus Planner 3 | Checks, evaluation and bounded revisions |
# | Planner 4 minus Planner 1 | The complete workflow compared with the baseline |
# 
# For every binary comparison, we count cases where both planners succeed, only the later planner succeeds, only the earlier planner succeeds, or neither succeeds. Rate differences are reported in **percentage points**. We compare the complete implementations; their different numbers of calls and supporting operations prevent us from treating every difference as the isolated effect of one feature.
# 
# ### How we describe uncertainty
# 
# We use the planned **2,000 paired group resamples with seed 42**. Each resample draws groups with replacement and keeps every eligible member, including repeated contributions when a group is drawn more than once. The same draws are used for the matched planners or classifiers. We recalculate the original record-level measure using the actual resampled denominator.
# 
# For planners, the main grouping uses the adopted profile families. All 150 feasible profiles happen to have different family IDs. We also retain the separately planned wording-pattern sensitivity. The original 14-pattern mapping contains three groups in the feasible subset; we do not replace the adopted-family analysis with that alternative.
# 
# The classifier analysis uses the **438 original test records, 385 exercise groups and all 16 classes**. We read the saved predictions from Step 32 and calculate macro-F1, accuracy, class-level results and the three fixed classifier contrasts. Classes absent from a resample remain on the frozen class list with the recorded zero-division convention. The validation-selected application classifier remains unchanged.
# 
# We save the actual draws, estimates and individual 95% percentile intervals. The classifier's numerical conventions were already frozen. For planner details not specified earlier, we record the numerical implementation explicitly: PCG64, sorted exact group IDs and linear percentile endpoints. We do not claim those extra numerical details were chosen before the final planner results were available.
# 
# These intervals describe changes in the mix of our existing benchmark groups. They do not measure training-seed variation, other language models, real-athlete outcomes or every source of scoring uncertainty. We do not introduce a significance test or declare a winner from an interval alone.
# 
# ### How we use retrieval and team review
# 
# We preserve the saved retrieval populations: **20 supported, two partially supported and eight unsupported questions**. The original ranking measure uses 24 questions with nonempty relevance sets. Six empty sets do not receive invented recall. We report the supported-only and other recorded slices separately, and distinguish ranked evidence coverage from the content actually delivered to a planner.
# 
# The Step 34 review covers **160 replies from 40 selected profiles**, with five separate review categories. It is one assisted review under Sam's direction. We keep its higher, equal and lower paired ratings as supplementary findings; we do not invent independent teammate ratings, estimate reviewer agreement, average the categories, or turn them into a new overall score. The selected sample has more challenge cases than the full benchmark, so its pooled ratings do not represent all 800 replies.
# 
# ### Running time, failures and interpretation
# 
# We show timing for all terminal workflows, including failed work, and separately identify completed replies and matched timing comparisons. Where the original journals are available, we also report model submissions, transport attempts, logical calls, stage usage and reference preparation. A final reply's token count is not treated as the cost of an entire multi-stage workflow. Missing hardware or dollar-cost measurements remain unavailable.
# 
# Our automatic goal check is still unimplemented and resource coverage remains limited. All-zero full-verification results therefore need that qualification. A constant zero bootstrap interval does not prove that all plans are equally good, equally poor or unusable. We keep genuine violations, missing proof and useful partial content distinct.
# 
# The code below checks the saved inputs and records versioned tables, figures, uncertainty calculations, failure findings and a readable report. It makes no new model requests. The report states which components are verified and which measurements remain unavailable. Step 36 will check that this saved analysis can be reproduced through a documented run.
# 

# In[28]:


