# <hr style="border: none; height: 10px; background-color: #FFD700;">

# ## 37. Results, Limitations, and Future Improvements
# 
# We bring together our classifier, reference-search, planner and team-review findings to explain what our prototype achieved and where it still needs work. We use the saved analysis from Step 35 and the successful reproduction check from Step 36, so the discussion stays connected to the results we actually recorded.
# 
# ### What we discuss
# 
# Our project has two different modeling tasks. The exercise classifier predicts an exercise category from its description. The basketball planners use the same local language model, `llama3.1:8b`, in four workflows: the model alone, the model with references, references with specialist roles, and a workflow with checks and revisions. We discuss these tasks separately because a useful exercise classifier does not establish that a generated weekly plan meets an athlete's needs.
# 
# For the planners, we compare response format, completed workflows, correctly formatted plan responses, timing and the original request checks. We keep those measures separate. A response can have the right structure and still change an existing practice, schedule too many sessions or leave out a requested answer.
# 
# We also explain the limits of our evidence. The automatic goal check is unfinished, and resource checks cover only some activity descriptions. At the same time, our saved assessments contain actual scheduling and request errors. We report both the checking gaps and the observed errors rather than treat every unresolved check as a confirmed failure of the plan itself.
# 
# ### How we interpret the findings
# 
# We use our fictional benchmark to compare the four complete workflows. We do not use it to claim improved athletic performance or to select a generally superior planner. The team-review section keeps the recorded assisted-review method and its limitations visible. Reference-search scores describe the saved ranking task; they do not prove that a planner used the evidence correctly.
# 
# Our improvement priorities follow the problems we observed: preserve existing commitments with explicit scheduling logic, check approved drill requirements, return food values directly from verified lookups, complete the missing checks and simplify the planning workflow. Any changes based on the final results will need a new experiment and fresh evaluation cases.
# 
# ### Saved discussion
# 
# The cell prepares a readable report and a Markdown copy for our project documentation. It reads the saved results, checks that Step 36 reproduced the same analysis and records the sources used for the discussion. It does not train models, generate plans or change our original scores. Rerunning it with the same inputs reuses the same report.
# 

